@inject ApiMetadata Api
@* SplitButton's Overview, written by hand after Button's: the main action and its menu, links and
   downloads, icon only, its looks and states, OnClick, a Toolbar, and what it does without script.
   Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<TextBlock>A split button is a button with a main action and, beside it, an arrow that opens a menu of related ones. It suits a set of actions where one is the usual one; when none is, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/menu">Menu</Anchor>. Clicking the main button only runs its action, and the arrow only opens the menu.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">The main action and its menu</Heading>
<TextBlock>The main button acts like a <Anchor Href="docs/components/button">Button</Anchor>: to post a form, set <Code>Submit</Code>, <Code>Name</Code> and <Code>Value</Code>. For the other choices, put <Code>MenuItem</Code>s inside the SplitButton.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="split-button-usage" />
<TextBlock>Choosing an item runs that item, and the main action stays as it was. The SplitButton doesn't remember the last choice. To make it the default, change <Code>Label</Code> and <Code>Href</Code> or <Code>OnClick</Code> yourself.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Links and downloads</Heading>
<TextBlock>The main button can be a link, such as to a page or a download. To make the main button a link, set <Code>Href</Code>; to open the link in a new tab, set <Code>External</Code> too. Other attributes, such as <Code>download</Code>, go to the main button.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="split-button-links" />

<Heading Level="2">Only an icon</Heading>
<TextBlock>An icon-only split button shows the main button's icon without its text. Set <Code>IconOnly</Code> with a <Code>StartIcon</Code>. The <Code>Label</Code> stays as the button's name and tooltip.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="split-button-icon-only" />

<Heading Level="2">Colors, weights and sizes</Heading>
<TextBlock>The accent, variant and size style both parts together: set <Code>Accent</Code>, <Code>Variant</Code> and <Code>Size</Code>. To make the main button fill the container, set <Code>FullWidth</Code>. Any of these you leave unset come from a <Anchor Href="docs/components/button-group">ButtonGroup</Anchor> or <Anchor Href="docs/components/toolbar">Toolbar</Anchor> around it.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="split-button-looks" />

<Heading Level="2">In a row of buttons</Heading>
<TextBlock>A split button can join a row of other buttons. Put it in a <Anchor Href="docs/components/button-group">ButtonGroup</Anchor>. Only the row's outer ends are rounded, and the SplitButton gets the group's look.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="split-button-in-group" />

<Heading Level="2">States</Heading>
<TextBlock>Either part can be disabled on its own, or the whole control at once. When there's no usual action but the others still work, set <Code>MainDisabled</Code>; when there's nothing else to choose, set <Code>MenuDisabled</Code>. To disable the whole control, or show it loading, set <Code>Disabled</Code> or <Code>Loading</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="split-button-states" />

<Heading Level="2">Where the menu opens</Heading>
<TextBlock>By default, the menu lines up with the start edge of the whole button. To open it elsewhere, set <Code>Placement</Code>. For long items, set <Code>PanelWidth</Code>, as on a Menu.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="split-button-panel-width" />

<Heading Level="2">Running C# on click</Heading>
<TextBlock>On <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">interactive pages</Anchor>, the main button and each <Code>MenuItem</Code> can run C# when clicked. Set <Code>OnClick</Code> on each.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="split-button-on-click" />

<Heading Level="2">In a Toolbar</Heading>
<TextBlock>In a <Anchor Href="docs/components/toolbar">Toolbar</Anchor>, use <Code>ToolbarSplitButton</Code>. It accepts a toolbar item's <Code>Position</Code>, <Code>Priority</Code> and <Code>Overflow</Code>. In the … menu, it becomes a submenu named by <Code>MenuLabel</Code>, with the main action as its first item, then the same items.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>With the toolbar's <Code>CollapseLabels</Code>, the main button drops its text and keeps its icon. <Anchor Href="docs/components/toolbar/actions#ex-toolbar-split-button">See it on the Toolbar's page</Anchor>, and narrow the window to see the items move.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@InToolbar</Code>

<Heading Level="2">With JavaScript off</Heading>
<TextBlock>With JavaScript off, the arrow still opens and closes the menu, and links and form posts work on the main button and on the items, as on a Menu. Alt+Down and the arrow keys require IzzyBlazor's script; without it, Tab to the arrow and press Enter. <Code>OnClick</Code> runs only on interactive pages.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>The arrow has a name for screen readers, and a loading button and a link that opens in a new tab have words of their own. To set them for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>MenuLabel</Code></td><td>The arrow's name and tooltip, and the submenu's name in a Toolbar's … menu</td><td>"{Label}: more options"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>LoadingLabel</Code></td><td>What screen readers hear while <Code>Loading</Code> is set</td><td>"Loading"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>NewTabLabel</Code></td><td>What screen readers hear after an external link's text</td><td>"(opens in a new tab)"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>

@if (OverviewParts.Of(Component, Api).Count > 0)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Parts</Heading>
}
<OverviewParts Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null || ApiComponent.Keyboard is { Count: > 0 })
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    @if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
    {
        <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
    }
    @KeyboardTable.Section(ApiComponent)
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string InToolbar = """
        <Toolbar Label="Document">
            <ToolbarSplitButton Label="Save" StartIcon="Lucide.Save" Submit MenuLabel="More save options" Priority="1">
                <MenuItem Submit Name="intent" Value="draft">Save as draft</MenuItem>
            </ToolbarSplitButton>
        </Toolbar>
        """;
}
